Patch 1 (see patches/ and the "MANTLE PATCH" markers inside upstream/pocketpy.h): ~15 lines,
forward-declaring three allocator hook functions (MantlePocketPy_Malloc/Realloc/Free,
implemented in PocketPyLib/Private/MantlePocketPyAlloc.c) so PK_MALLOC/PK_REALLOC/PK_FREE --
defined as function-like macros by PocketPyLib.Build.cs, pocketpy's own documented extension
point for a custom allocator -- have a visible C11 prototype before pocketpy.c's translation
unit uses them. PocketPy's public API has no *runtime* allocator-callback parameter (unlike
Luau's `lua_newstate(allocator, ud)`), only this compile-time macro seam, so a prototype has to
live somewhere in scope; pocketpy.h (included at the top of pocketpy.c) is that place. No other
line of pocketpy.h was touched for this patch. Identical in shape to the lab's own patch,
renamed Lab* -> Mantle* (the include is now "MantlePocketPyHooks.h", not "PocketPyHooks.h").

Patch 2 (see patches/ and the "MANTLE PATCH" markers inside upstream/pocketpy.c's
VM__run_top_frame, at the __NEXT_STEP label): ~10 lines, a deterministic per-opcode
instruction-budget interrupt. NOT part of the lab's own vendoring -- added after the hosted
integration build (2026-09-20) showed a runaway `while True: total = total + 1` Python script
freeze the whole game thread on frame 0. MantlePocketPy.cpp originally metered budget through
py_sys_settrace's TRACE_EVENT_LINE, which only fires on a *source line* change; that loop's
bytecode never crossed one, so the budget never fired, and even when a trace-hook-raised
exception did occur nothing checked for it before the next opcode ran. The patch increments
MantlePocketPy_OpCount[vm] every opcode and polls MantlePocketPy_Interrupt (a host-installed
function pointer, both declared in Public/MantlePocketPyHooks.h) once every
MANTLE_POCKETPY_INTERRUPT_PERIOD opcodes; a false return raises a RuntimeError and
`goto __ERROR` right there, in the same opcode, the same way the existing PK_ENABLE_WATCHDOG
block a few lines below it already does for a wall-clock timeout. py_sys_settrace is no longer
called by MantlePocketPy.cpp at all (it costs a source-location lookup on every opcode).

A separate generator patch was considered and rejected, carried over unchanged from the lab's own evaluation:
a `pk_generator_send` entry point so a suspended generator could be resumed with a value (to
support CPython-style `x = yield expr`). `yield` is a STATEMENT in PocketPy's grammar, not an
expression -- `x = yield expr` does not parse at all (only bare `yield expr` and `yield from
expr` statements do). MantlePocketPy.cpp's task/signal waiting never needs a value fed back
through the yield expression in the first place; see its "Wait tokens" section for the exact
idiom scripts use instead (`t = task.wait(1); yield t; elapsed = t.result`). Plain `py_next()`
(equivalent to CPython's `gen.send(None)`) is sufficient to drive every generator this binding
uses.

Not vendored / not enabled: no threading, no os/socket modules, no `open()`. PK_ENABLE_OS=0
excludes filesystem and socket code from the build entirely (compile-time guarantee, not a
runtime check), matching the sandboxing shape LuauLib and QuickJSLib already use.

